<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Province of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1816">1816</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> formed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> from lands taken from the Kingdom of Saxony and older Prussian territories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>. Its capital was Magdeburg. It was divided in 1944, and its lands now lie mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/province-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Province of Saxony" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Provinz_Sachsen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, also called Prussian Saxony, was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> and later the Free State of Prussia from 1816 until 1944. It was formed from territories ceded or returned to Prussia in 1815 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>: most of the northern territories of the Kingdom of Saxony, the former French Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a>, the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a> and some other districts.</p><p>The Prussian lands beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a>, the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wernigerode">Wernigerode</a> and the western part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, had belonged to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> from 1807 to 1813 before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> regained them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> territory gained after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in 1813, confirmed in 1815, included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a>, Mansfeld, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a> and Henneberg. Lands given to Prussia after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Reichsdeputationshauptschluss</a> included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichsfeld">Eichsfeld</a>, the former imperial cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhlhausen">Mühlhausen</a> and Nordhausen, and Quedlinburg Abbey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The province had an irregular shape. It surrounded enclaves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and some Ernestine duchies, had several exclaves, and was almost bisected by the Duchy of Anhalt. Its majority was Protestant, with a Catholic minority of about 8 per cent in 1905. It sent 20 members to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> and 38 delegates to the Prussian House of Representatives. It was one of the richest regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, with highly developed agriculture and industry. Before 1944 it was divided into three administrative regions, the Regierungsbezirke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>. In 1932 it was enlarged with the regions around Ilfeld and Elbingerode, formerly part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-hanover">Province of Hanover</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 1 July 1944 the province was divided along the lines of its three regions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> region was merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkalden">Schmalkalden</a> district of Hesse-Nassau and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> region became the Province of Magdeburg, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> region became the Province of Halle-Merseburg. In 1945 the Soviet military administration combined Magdeburg and Halle-Merseburg with the State of Anhalt into the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, with Halle as its capital, and in 1947 it became a state.</p><p>The East German states were abolished in 1952 and recreated in 1990 as the modern states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>. The old borders lasted longest in the church, since the Church Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in the Evangelical Church remained in existence until 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Province of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1815 it returned to Prussia and formed the core of the <b>Province of Saxony</b>, with Magdeburg as provincial capital, a role it now holds in Saxony-Anhalt.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-magdeburg">Duchy of Magdeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1945 it was occupied and placed in the Soviet zone, where it was merged with the Prussian <b>Province of Saxony</b> to form the province, later Land, of Saxony-Anhalt.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-anhalt">Free State of Anhalt</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna returned the city to Prussia in 1815, as part of the <b>Province of Saxony</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-erfurt">Principality of Erfurt</a></li><li><blockquote>It returned to Prussia in 1815 and became part of the <b>Province of Saxony</b>; the town is now in Saxony-Anhalt.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-halberstadt">Principality of Halberstadt</a></li></ul></section>
